/*
 * Releases the memory of a result that a function of this API allocated
 * (char** out_result...). NULL is accepted. Use this function, never free():
 * the library may use another C runtime than its caller (Windows).
 */
{{ P }}_API void {{ m.free_function }}(void* memory);

/*
 * Callbacks. A std::function parameter is passed as three C parameters: a
 * callback, its user_data and a release function. The callback is called with
 * user_data first, then the arguments, then (for a non-void result) a pointer
 * to the result, which is value-initialized beforehand (0, false): a callback
 * that does not write it gives that value. It may be called on any thread and
 * must not throw nor longjmp. Pointer arguments are only valid during the call.
 * Unless release is NULL, release(user_data) is called exactly once, on any
 * thread, when C++ no longer holds the callback: possibly before the function
 * returns, and also when the function fails. A NULL callback is an empty
 * std::function.
 *
 * {{ m.disable_callbacks }}(release): from now on, the callbacks given with
 * this release function are neither called (C++ gets their default result)
 * nor released. For language runtimes that shut down while C++ may still hold
 * their callbacks (in globals, or in objects that are never destroyed). Every
 * release function that was disabled stays disabled, so that several runtimes
 * can share the library (sub-interpreters, other languages). NULL is ignored.
 *
 * {{ m.enable_callbacks }}(release): undoes it. A runtime calls it when it
 * starts, with the release function it will pass, in case an earlier runtime
 * that was disabled at exit had a function at the same address.
 */
{{ P }}_API void {{ m.disable_callbacks }}(void (*release)(void*));
{{ P }}_API void {{ m.enable_callbacks }}(void (*release)(void*));
